Upgrading from v2.71?
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    You may notice some of the CPS files located in directories OTHER than
the "cps/" directory. These stragglers should be deleted when found. You can
use a command like the following in an attempt to find the stragglers:

            list base0:#?/#?0D lformat %P%N

    The above would look inside every directory located in BASE0: for files
ending in "0D", which is what the CPS files DO end with. If any are found,
they'll be listed below the run of this command. You can then go into the
given directories and delete them. If ONLY cps files are listed, you could
also issue this command...

            delete base0:#?/#?0D

    ...to have your Amiga do the work for you, but make SURE it was only
CPS files that were listed, just in case you might have OTHER files that end
in the "0D" characters on your drives.

NOTE: Starting in v2.71, the command file, readme and history files for this
      program are ALSO stored inside the given "doors:cps/" directory. If you
      are using an older version, please DELETE these three files from your
      "doors:" path directly. You can now CALL the "cps/" directory anything
      you'd like. You're no longer locked into using "cps/"...

    This is that neat program that comes up right after a user either uploads
or downloads a file to your system. This program differs from my previous
contribution in this field "CPS_Graph", in that it not only displays a BAR
Graph representation of the user's CPS Rate, but also displays the CPS Rate
in BIG Numbers.

    My main qualm with "Brazilian"'s & "Pharaoh"'s work was that it didn't
adequately support the NonANSI user. The "term type" was captured in the
beginning of the file, but it wasn't utilized ANYWHERE in the program.

    In the original of this program, the "high baud rate records" were saved
directly to the DOORS: directory. I thought this was rather silly, so I
decided to use the same DOORS:CPS/ directory that my "CPS_Graph" Convert
used. This means if you were using my "CPS_Graph" program, then this program
will also be able to read and write from the SAME HighCPS rate files.

    The other little change I made was to increase the CPS Percentage Rates
to 125%, since a lot of users are capable of u/d'ing at ABOVE their normal
cps rate. Again, I stole from my previous "CPS_Graph" program.

    Lastly, I decided to stick with my new philosophy of "SysOp Definability"
by giving the SysOp 16 separate variables that control what COLORS to use
when displaying the display screen. There are also 2 TEST Variables the SysOp
can use to "try out" certain color settings, without getting the "0 CPS" rate
if run as a regular Arexx file. There are also 4 "environment" variables that
will let you adjust HOW the program does it's work.

    The 2 test variables are t1 and t2. When you wish this program to be
used by the bbs within BBSTEXT, you MUST make sure t1 is set to 0 (ZERO),
or the program will be locked in TEST MODE, and will NOT give valid results.

    If t1 is set to 1, the program can be run from your DOORS area as a
regular PFile. A random CPS rate will be chosen for use in displaying the
graph and BIG Number display. You can use this mode to try out different
combinations of colors to find the one you like best.

    The t2 variable can be used to display the ASCII Equivalent of this
screen, instead of the ANSI screen. Set it to 1 to see your current colors
using the ASCII screen.

    Just remember, when you're satisfied with the color combination, MAKE
SURE TO SET "t1" EQUAL TO 0 (ZERO).

    Ok, so how do you set this thing up? Here's all you do:

1: Copy the "CPS/" directory into your DOORS: directory.

2: Add the following to the END of Line 576 in your BBSTEXT file:

                {#0doors:cps/BIG_CPS_Speed}
                ^
            Control-Q

   This addition should come BEFORE the "\n1" you see at the END of the line,
so when your done, it should look like this:

The average CPS rate for the last transfer was %d.{#0pfiles:BIG_CPS_Speed}\n1
                                                  ^                       ^
                                              Control-Q            Control-Y

NOTE:   If you're presently using either my "CPS_Graph" or another such
        program, you may see it's load command here. You should replace
        any such load code you might see with the above, unless for some
        odd reason you actually WANTED to use more than one of these mods
        at the same time.

3: If, as the above NOTE suggests, you are presently using either "CPS_Graph",
   or the "BIG_CPS_Speed, v1.0", then you will ALSO need to DELETE all files
   found in the "DOORS:CPS/" directory, as this new version adds a DATE Stamp
   to each high score file as well as the other info.

4: That's it! Your done!

    When the next user either uploads or downloads something from your bbs,
and provided they didn't state they wanted to "Auto-Logoff", then this mod
should present the user with a neat graphic screen.

    After the initial run, you'll notice inside the "cps/" directory files 
named 30, 120, 240, 960, 1440, etc. dependent upon which baud rate accessed 
this file. These "baud" files hold the info on the user who holds the 
HIGHEST CPS Rate for that Baud rate. To reset any baud, simply delete its
file. To reset the WHOLE thing, delete those files using a NUMBER as their
filename. New files will be created the next time the program is accessed!

v2.0 Extended Notes:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    There are 3 additional SysOp Definable Variables you may wish to edit
before using this version for the first time. They are located after the
color variables near the beginning of the "BIG_CPS_Speed" Arexx Command
file. Load this file into a text editor...

V1 - Number of Days until HighCPS file can be RESET. Default=7 (Days)
        - This is a helpful variable, because it allows the "record" to be
          RESET every so often. This way, the same user won't be hogging
          the record all the time.

V2 - MINIMUM KB before BIG_CPS_Speed will be used.   Default=50 (KiloBytes)
        - Set this to the number of KILOBYTES the file must AT LEAST be
          in length, BEFORE the CPS rate can be used to compete against the
          existing Record CPS. Other CPS-Listers don't allow for this, which
          can result in HUGE CPS Rates being used as the "record", because
          a user up/downloaded a small text file or something similar. Now,
          YOU put the CAP on the MINIMUM length!

V3 - Should "U" and "D" HighCPS Files be used?       Default=1 (1=Yes,0=No)
        - This is a TOGGLE Variable, and should be set to either 1 or 0.
          It's very possible that a DIFFERENT CPS rate could be gotten if
          a file was UPLOADED, instead of DOWNLOADED, or vice-versa. Even if
          it was the SAME file at the SAME byte length, dependent upon the
          CPU Sending & the CPU Receiving, the CPS Rates could differ.
        - Stating "0" (for NO) here will use the same files the earlier 1.0
          version did. That is, the "240", "960", "1440", etc files.
        - Stating "1" (for YES) will create a separate file for each baud
          rate for UPLOADS and DOWNLOADS, so you'd have "240U" (Upload),
          "240D" (Download), etc.

V4 - Use the Segmented, or Open Box?          Default=1 (1=Segmented,0=Open)
        - This variable allows you to change the look of the box the CPS
          Bar Graph will appear on. Set to "1", and the familiar 2.0/2.1
          Segmented Box will be used, while set to "0" and a new "open"
          formatted box will be used instead. You can try changing the value
          of this variable while in TEST mode to get an idea of what each
          BOX shape looks like.

V5 - You should NOT modify this variable unless you hear from Dotoran that
     it needs to be changed. This is the OFFSET value for the LAST COMMAND
     that was entered from the BBS. It is used for deciding which Log file
     needs to be read. Because it is a LONG Offset Value (7-Digits), it's
     possible this value may CHANGE if the CNet "C" structures ever change
     again in a future version. In this event, all you'll have to do is to
     change this value to the NEW OFFSET value for that version and this
     program should continue to work flawlessly!

How It Works:
~~~~~~~~~~~~
    This version of BIG_CPS_Speed reads CPS Rates from either the "uloads"
or "dloads" logs in your "SysData:Log/" directory, INSTEAD of simply reading
the LAST CPS Rate found in Memory as v1.0 did.

    The appropriate log is read, then a BACKWARDS search is done of each
entry IN the log, until an entry from another user is found.

    Each entry that is read has it's file length looked at. If this length
exceeds the value found in the "V2" variable, then it's CPS Rate is also
compared to the last CPS Rate(or 0) and the HIGHEST of the two CPS Rates
is remembered.

    Entries whose file lengths are equal to or lower than the "V2" variable
are skipped, as are entries whose lengths MAY be long enough, but whose CPS
Rate is equal to or lower than the HIGHEST CPS Rate currently remembered.

    When ALL entries in the log under the current user have been looked at,
the remembered CPS Rate is sent back to the main routine. If this CPS Rate
is still set to 0, this would mean that ALL the files in question were BELOW
the stated "V2" value, so the file would exit without printing anything.

    If, however, the CPS Rate is NOT 0, then yet another check is done using
the current HighCPS Record value. If the CPS Rate is Higher than the Record,
then this user becomes the NEW Record Holder.

    It is not necessary to delete the "uloads" or "dloads" logs upon the
completion of this file, because of the way this file is written. Even if
the SAME user is the NEXT user to UPLOAD or DOWNLOAD(which would mean his
NEW Log entries would immediately follow his OLD entries), they would STILL
have to BETTER their BEST CPS Rate. Why? Because the user must BEAT the CPS
Rate on Record, NOT simply MEET it. This was done to insure the same user
couldn't simply reset the RESET Date by obtaining the SAME HighCPS Rate. If,
indeed, they can BETTER their speed, then by all means, reset the RESET date.

    I took some time playing around with this routine using a friends BBS,
GOD How I love the SHELL and ID Commands, and wasn't able to find fault in
this routine. I DID, however, notice that HIGH CPS Rates were also given to
50+K TEXT Files that had NOT been packed, as you might find in the GFiles
Section. I even tried downloading an unpacked 230K Text file and STILL was
given a CPS Rate of 1860 at 14400 Baud, so there WILL still sometimes be a
VERY HIGH CPS Rate on file. That's why I also included the RESET Date "V1"
variable.

    Lastly, some SysOps were telling me that the CPS Rate stated by CNet &
the CPS Rate stated by BIG_CPS_Speed differed. The only time the figures will
MATCH would be if only ONE file were uploaded or downloaded. If multiple files
are sent/received, the CPS Rate stated by CNet is the AVERAGE of ALL CPS Rates
obtained for ALL files, while the BIG_CPS_Speed Rate will only be the HIGHEST
CPS Rate obtained from ANY ONE of the up/downloaded files.

    If you find fault with this program, you should know by now what to do!

